entries.english:
Adam Friedrich [von Seinsheim], Bischof zu Würzburg, grants Johann Friedrich Karl Philipp, son of the late Adalbert Friedrich von Rosenberg, episcopal councillor and official at Arnstein, one half of the following properties as male fiefs:
- Thundorf Castle with all rights, appurtenances, and revenues, except only the manor farm;
- the deserted settlement of Ransbach (Ramspach), with its mill and farm, and all rights and revenues;
- the villages of Theinfeld and Rothhausen, with all rights, possessions, and revenues there;
- an estate at Maßbach behind the churchyard;
- the properties at Poppenlauer;
- the properties at Ebertshausen;
- half the tithe at Altenmünster (Münster auf dem Schlattach);
- one third of the tithe at Oberlauringen;
- one eighth of the tithe at Alsleben;
- one quarter of the tithe at Ober- and Untereßfeld;
- a vineyard and two woodlands at Thundorf.

The bishop also grants them the following properties, formerly held as allodial property by Veit Ulrich von Schaumberg, who then enfeoffed them to the Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg:
- the manor farm and vineyards at Thundorf;
- the lake at Weißensee;
- a fishery in the Lauer below Maßbach;
- a fishery in the Lauer in the district of Rothhausen;
- three quarters of the mill at Rothhausen;
- two estates at Theinfeld;
- two freehold estates at Weichtungen;
- four rent fiefs at Stadtlauringen;
- nine estates and eleven fiefs at Maßbach;
- the Brand mill below Maßbach;
- five estates and four fiefs at Poppenlauer;
- three estates and one fief at Nüdlingen (Niedlingen);
- one fief at Rannungen;
- a field estate at Hesselbach;
- the Herlingsberg woodland;
- the male fief at Prappach.
These properties were purchased from the brothers Georg Dietrich, Wilhelm Ludwig, Wilhelm Ulrich, Georg Wilhelm, Johann Sigmund, Johann Philipp, and Johann Wilhelm von Schaumberg.

Finally, the bishop grants them the following properties, originally held as fiefs from the County of Henneberg and then the Duchy of Saxe-Weimar, which passed to Würzburg as a result of a treaty between the Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg and the Duchy of Saxe-Weimar:
- the cent court at Maßbach, subsequently moved to Poppenlauer, with all appurtenances and rights, excluding the villages of Maßbach and Volkershausen;
- half the Vogtei at Poppenlauer;
- two estates at Maßbach and a meadow between Maßbach and Rothhausen;
- one quarter of the tithe at Zeil am Main;
- one third of a small quarter of the wine tithe at Zeil am Main;
- one third and one quarter of a whole quarter of the wine tithe at Zeil am Main;
- four woodlands near Maßbach;
- the wine rents and interest revenues at Schonungen. If the von Rosenbach family should ever have no male heirs, the Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg is obliged to pay the family's female heirs 2,000 Reichstaler in return for these revenues.

The other half of the fiefs is held by his uncle Hans Philipp von Rosenberg.
Sealed: the bishop, with his secret seal.

Notes: abstract (18th century).
